## Numbered findings (F = factual/consequential, C = cosmetic/style)
F1 Grace period misstated. Text (Who Can Sponsor...): "If business growth pushes your headcount past 100 qualifying workers, the IRS provides a two-year grace period to maintain the plan or transition to another option." IRS FAQ: "If you previously maintained a SIMPLE IRA plan, you satisfy the 100-employee limitation for the 2 calendar years immediately following the calendar year for which you last satisfied the 100-employee limitation." Replace with that rule; drop "or transition to another option".
F2 Inherited contradiction of F1 (blocks, affects the revised answer). Text (When to switch...): "you've grown past 100 employees (which disqualifies you from SIMPLE IRA eligibility)". Contradicts the new grace-period section and the IRS FAQ. Replace the parenthetical with "(SIMPLE IRA eligibility ends after the two-year grace period that follows your last qualifying year)".
F3 Early-withdrawal FAQ misstates the age-55 exception. Text: "A 401(k) also allows penalty-free withdrawals if you leave your employer at age 55 or older, an exception that does not apply to a SIMPLE IRA." IRS table: "the employee separates from service during or after the year the employee reaches age 55"; it waives the 10% additional tax only (ordinary income tax still applies) and covers money from the plan of the employer you leave. Rewrite from that. Same answer says "25% early withdrawal penalty ... dropping to 10%": the IRS term is additional tax; use it consistently.
F4 Same error in the withdrawal section. Text: "The age-55 separation rule marks another major distinction, allowing penalty-free distributions from a 401(k) if you leave or lose your job in the calendar year you turn 55 or older." Wrong timing ("during or after the year"), "penalty-free" implies tax-free, "marks another major distinction" is filler. Plain rewrite: "A 401(k) participant who separates from service during or after the year they turn 55 does not owe the 10% additional tax on money from that employer's plan, though the withdrawal is still taxed as income."
F5 Rollover consequence drops the condition. Text: "The IRS treats any rollover to a 401(k) or a Traditional IRA during this period as an early distribution, which triggers ordinary income tax and the full 25% penalty." IRS: you include the amount in gross income and pay the additional 25% tax "unless you qualify for an exception". Add "unless an exception applies".
F6 Overstatement. Text: "a standard 401(k) never requires the business to match employee deferrals." A plan document that adopts a match formula obliges the employer to follow it; the page's own earlier wording ("isn't legally required") is the supportable claim. Also the inherited table/section ignore the top-heavy minimum contribution for a standard 401(k); advisory. Replace "never requires" with "does not legally require".
F7 Unsupported sentence. Text: "That comparison explains how an individual account avoids the mandatory employer contribution structures and the two-year 25% penalty window." Not on the closed fact list; describes another page rather than stating a sourced fact. Delete it, or state only that early distributions from a traditional IRA face the 10% additional tax unless an exception applies (F1).
F8 One-plan rule paraphrase is looser than the IRS and conflicts with the FAQ. Text: "an employer maintaining a SIMPLE IRA generally cannot sponsor any other retirement plan in the same calendar year. That restriction blocks a business owner from running a SIMPLE IRA and a 401(k) at the same time." IRS: "you can't contribute to a SIMPLE IRA plan for a calendar year if you maintain another retirement plan and any of your employees receives an allocation or accrues a benefit under the other plan." The existing FAQ says "for the same employees". Align both with the IRS wording. Low-moderate.
F9 Eligibility paraphrase omits the two-year test. Text: "you cannot exclude eligible part-time staff who clear that $5,000 mark." Eligibility needs $5,000 in any two prior years and an expected $5,000 this year. Low.
F10 "Standard penalty exceptions, including disability and a beneficiary of a deceased owner, apply equally to both plans." The IRS table shows several exceptions differ by plan type (first-home purchase IRA only; QDRO and separation plans only). Say "Some exceptions, such as disability and a beneficiary of a deceased owner, apply to both plans." Low.
C1 Coy abstraction "the company" (hard fail under anti-AI GATE 1): FAQ "With advance notice, the company can also lower that match" and eligibility "if the company selected the flat contribution option". Use "the employer".
C2 Reader-assumption: FAQ "Your employer can choose between..." and withdrawal section "your employer deposits the first contribution into your SIMPLE IRA", on a page for owners choosing a plan. Scope to employee vs owner ("an employer can choose", "the employer deposits ... into the employee's SIMPLE IRA").
C3 Overloaded sentences (>30 words, 2+ ideas): withdrawal opener (~47 words); verdict sentence 1 (~50 words, lengthened by this run); intro sentence 1 (~40 words); employer section paragraph 1 sentence 1 (~43); "An employee who holds two jobs..." (35); "Neither plan serves as an effective holding place..." (31, placeholder noun "holding place").
C4 Meta-narration and filler: "administrative costs covered across this guide" (page self-reference); "marks another major distinction".
C5 monotone-rhythm: in the new withdrawal, eligibility and employer sections, 31 of 35 sentences are 15+ words (2 short, 2 medium). The first 11 sentences of the withdrawal section are all long (about 47, 18, 20, 29, 17, 27, 25, 30, 30, 17, 24 words) and a later run of 10 (38, 23, 26, 30, 24, 17, 19, 23, 23, 18) is also single-band, which breaks the 10-sentence rule. Vary real sentences; do not pad or stub.
C6 Section openers not self-contained (AEO): "Who must be included follows IRS rules: any employee who..." and the disconnected sentence "A growing business that moves to a 401(k) makes that switch effective January 1." (does not connect to the one-plan rule).
C7 Mechanical: body link and Sources entry use .../retirement-topics-tax-on-early-distributions, which 301s to .../retirement-topics-exceptions-to-tax-on-early-distributions. Update both (and the closed URL list).
C8 Takeaway placement: the SIMPLE-vs-401(k) withdrawal consequence (25% window, age-55 only for 401(k)) is not in the verdict. No contradiction, so the intent/takeaway check passes, but the conditional takeaway sits in an overloaded last paragraph of the withdrawal section.

